 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A weekly estimate varies substantially. Which conclusion about collecting more observations is best?','More observations may improve precision if sampling remains appropriate, but cannot by itself remove systematic bias.',['A larger sample guarantees an unbiased estimate.','Week-to-week variation proves all data are wrong.','Any larger sample necessarily includes every affected group.'],'A larger sample can reduce random uncertainty, but biased selection or measurement can persist.','sample-size-precision','larger-sample-as-bias-cure'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A supervisor recommends clearer labels after users place documents in the wrong folders. Which assumption links the remedy to the problem?','Users can identify the correct folder more accurately when labels distinguish the categories.',['Folder color is the only source of error.','Every user ignores instructions.','Labels make record review unnecessary.'],'The proposed intervention depends on category confusion being a contributing cause.','labeling-mechanism','surface-fix-guarantees-result'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','An office pilots staggered appointments. Which comparison best checks crowding while also monitoring access?','Compare occupancy and waiting times across comparable periods, and track walk-ins and people unable to use the schedule.',['Count scheduled appointments only and treat nonattendance as resolved.','Compare one unusually busy day with a quiet day.','Measure staff preference without tracking user access.'],'Comparable periods help assess crowding, while walk-ins and excluded users reveal whether the change shifts rather than solves access problems.','appointment-pilot-measurement','attendance-as-access'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A reviewer recommends checking source documents after duplicate totals were found. Which assumption is most important?','The source documents can help identify how the duplicate entries arose.',['The totals are necessarily fraudulent.','Every source document is accurate.','A review should not retain evidence.'],'The recommendation relies on source records helping to diagnose the mismatch, though they too may need verification.','audit-trail-mechanism','source-as-infallible'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','If an application lacks a required attachment, it is returned for completion. Application P was returned. What follows?','The return alone does not prove the attachment was missing.',['The attachment was certainly missing.','The application was approved.','All returned applications lack attachments.'],'The rule states one reason for return, not the only possible reason.','conditional-sufficiency','affirming-consequent'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','Every authorized release has a logged approval. Record Q has a logged approval. What is valid?','The logged approval alone does not prove that the release was authorized.',['Record Q was released lawfully.','No release is logged.','Record Q is confidential.'],'A necessary feature of an authorized release is not necessarily sufficient to establish authorization.','necessary-vs-sufficient','affirming-consequent'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','No request without a tracking number can be monitored in the portal. Request X can be monitored in the portal. What follows?','Request X has a tracking number.',['Request X was approved.','Every numbered request is complete.','The portal created the request.'],'The stated necessary condition is present for a request that is monitorable.','necessary-condition-inference','necessary-condition-ignored'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','All branch offices submit a monthly report. Some units submit a monthly report. What can be inferred about those units?','They are not necessarily branch offices.',['Every unit is a branch office.','No branch office reports monthly.','All reporting units are branches.'],'The shared property does not show the units belong to the branch-office category.','category-inference','affirming-property'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A memo claims a new notice caused faster service but compares a holiday week with an ordinary week. What is the main analytic concern?','The periods may differ in workload or operating conditions, confounding the comparison.',['A notice cannot affect behavior.','Faster service is impossible to measure.','The holiday must have caused the change.'],'A period difference can introduce an alternative explanation; comparable periods are needed.','period-comparability','before-after-confounding'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A staff survey has a high response rate but excludes contract employees. What limitation remains?','Coverage bias may remain because an affected group was excluded.',['A high response rate proves full representation.','Contract employees cannot be relevant.','The responses are all invalid.'],'Response rate does not compensate for excluding a population segment.','survey-coverage','response-rate-as-representativeness'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','An improvement is reported using a single annual total, although monthly demand changes sharply. What additional presentation would help?','Monthly counts or rates with the relevant denominator.',['A larger title.','A second copy of the annual total.','A list of unrelated staff names.'],'A time-resolved measure can show whether the annual result masks seasonal or monthly changes.','temporal-resolution','aggregate-hides-pattern'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A unit reports 72 resolved cases out of 90 received. What is the resolution rate?','80%',['72%','18%','90%'],'72 ÷ 90 = 0.8, or 80%.','rate-calculation','numerator-as-percent'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','Two measures are reported: 14 errors among 280 entries and 18 among 600 entries. Which has the higher error rate?','14 among 280: 5%, compared with 3% for 18 among 600.',['18 among 600: 18%.','They are equal at 4%.','The larger error count always means a larger rate.'],'14/280 = 5%; 18/600 = 3%; compare ratios, not counts alone.','rate-comparison','count-rate-confusion'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','Office A resolved 42 cases in 6 days; Office B resolved 56 cases in 8 days. How do their average daily rates compare?','They are equal at 7 cases per day.',['Office A is 1 case/day faster.','Office B is 2 cases/day faster.','Their totals are equal.'],'42 ÷ 6 = 7 and 56 ÷ 8 = 7 cases per day.','equal-rates','totals-as-rate'],
 ['cse-data-trends','A weekly rate changes from 8% to 10%. By how many percentage points did it rise?','2 percentage points.',['2%.','25 percentage points.','18 percentage points.'],'Subtract the rates: 10% − 8% = 2 percentage points; the relative percent increase would be different.','percentage-points','percent-vs-points',4,false,['operation'=>'difference','inputs'=>[10,8],'expected'=>2]],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','Deadline is to time as filing key is to:','order.',['volume.','authority.','accuracy.'],'A deadline organizes an action in time; a filing key organizes records in a sequence.','organizational-analogy','surface-association'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','A hypothesis is to test as a draft is to:','review.',['archive permanently.','measure distance.','authorize payment.'],'A test evaluates a hypothesis; a review evaluates a draft.','evaluation-analogy','object-category'],
