 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','Some requests with receipts are incomplete. Request Z has a receipt. What can be concluded?','Request Z may be complete or incomplete.',['Request Z is incomplete.','Every request with a receipt is incomplete.','No request with a receipt is complete.'],'The existential statement does not classify this particular request.','existential-quantifier','some-to-all'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A record is retained if it is under appeal or subject to an audit hold. Record B is neither under appeal nor on audit hold. What follows under the rule?','The stated grounds for retention are absent.',['The record must be retained under the rule.','The record must be destroyed immediately.','An audit hold exists.'],'Neither disjunct is true, so these listed grounds do not establish retention; other rules may still apply.','disjunctive-condition','listed-ground-as-only-law'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A notice is valid only when dated and signed. It is dated but unsigned. What follows?','The stated validity condition is not satisfied.',['Dating alone makes it valid.','It is automatically void for every purpose.','The signature is optional under the premise.'],'Both required elements must be present; no broader legal outcome is stated.','conjunctive-condition','one-element-suffices'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A searchable index is introduced to reduce retrieval delays. Which evaluation would best test whether retrieval improved across different file types?','Compare retrieval time and failed searches before and after, stratified by indexed status and comparable request complexity.',['Count index entries without measuring retrieval.','Use only the fastest successful search after launch.','Ask whether staff like the index and treat preference as proof of faster retrieval.'],'Time, failures, file type, and task complexity help determine whether the observed change reflects retrieval performance rather than a narrow subset.','index-evaluation-design','fastest-case-as-general-result'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A manager proposes translating instructions after users misunderstand a process. What should be established?','Language comprehension is a relevant source of the misunderstanding for affected users.',['Translation resolves every process problem.','All users prefer one language.','The original instruction is legally incorrect.'],'The remedy is responsive only if language comprehension is part of the cause.','language-access','single-cause-assumption'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A report recommends a new check after a rise in duplicate entries. What additional assumption is necessary?','The check can detect duplicates before they affect the final record.',['Every duplicate is intentional.','A check guarantees no error ever occurs.','The rise is unrelated to the data process.'],'The control must operate at a point where it can identify the targeted error.','control-point','control-as-guarantee'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A unit compares raw complaint counts between two branches, but one serves twice as many users. What is the best next comparison?','Complaint rates using a comparable measure of users served.',['Raw totals without context.','The branch with more complaints is automatically worse.','The branch names in alphabetical order.'],'A denominator makes the comparison more meaningful when populations differ.','denominator-normalization','count-only-comparison'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A report claims a procedure is faster because the latest case took less time than the previous case. What is the main problem?','Two cases may not be representative or comparable.',['Time cannot be measured.','A procedure never affects duration.','One case always proves a trend.'],'A pair of cases without comparable conditions is weak evidence for a general speed claim.','sample-size-and-comparability','anecdote-as-trend'],
 ['cse-data-trends','A monthly count is 100, 90, 95, 80. Which statement is accurate?','The count fluctuates but ends below its initial value.',['It declines in every month.','It ends above its initial value.','It rises by five each month.'],'The sequence has an increase from 90 to 95, but 80 is below 100.','fluctuating-series','monotonic-assumption'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A report says 45 of 150 files were delayed. What fraction of the files were delayed in simplest form?','3/10',['1/3','3/5','30/150'],'45/150 reduces by dividing both values by 15, giving 3/10.','fraction-of-total','failure-to-reduce'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A section completed 56 of 70 scheduled reviews. What percentage were completed?','80%',['70%','14%','125%'],'56 ÷ 70 = 0.8, or 80%.','completion-rate','difference-as-rate'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','An office’s average wait falls from 30 minutes to 24 minutes. What is the absolute reduction?','6 minutes.',['6%.','20 minutes.','24%.'],'30 − 24 = 6 minutes.','absolute-change','absolute-as-relative'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A report states “the policy worked” after an outcome improved, but gives no comparison or alternative explanations. What is the most defensible revision?','The outcome improved during the period; the policy’s separate effect remains uncertain.',['The policy certainly caused the improvement.','The outcome did not improve.','The policy had no possible effect.'],'The observation is real as reported, but a causal statement needs stronger design or evidence.','causal-qualification','correlation-as-causation'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','A table is to organize as an index is to:','locate.',['authorize.','estimate.','translate.'],'A table organizes information; an index helps locate information.','functional-analogy','surface-association'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','A baseline is to comparison as a denominator is to:','rate.',['deadline.','signature.','sequence.'],'A baseline provides the reference for change; a denominator provides the base for a rate.','reference-basis-analogy','category-confusion'],
 ['cse-abstract-transformations','A symbol rule replaces every square with a circle and every circle with a triangle. What replaces a square under this rule?','A circle.',['A triangle.','A square.','A diamond.'],'Apply the stated one-step mapping: square becomes circle.','symbol-mapping','apply-second-step'],
 ['cse-sequence-inference','A sequence lists consecutive positive squares: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25. What is next?','36',['35','49','30'],'These are 1², 2², 3², 4², and 5²; the next is 6² = 36.','consecutive-squares','difference-rule-overfit'],
];
