$readingFinal=[
 ['The new intake form reduced missing contact fields. The office did not measure whether applicants needed more time to complete it.','Which limitation remains?','The form’s effect on completion time is unknown.',['The number of missing fields could not be counted.','Every applicant completed the form.','The reduction must be caused by longer completion time.'],'The report measures missing fields but gives no completion-time evidence.','reading-unmeasured-outcome','measured-one-outcome-as-all'],
 ['A branch reports a 10-minute median wait. Its longest waits occur on market days, which the monthly median does not show.','What additional view could reveal the pattern?','Wait-time distributions or period-specific medians.',['The monthly median repeated in the title.','The branch logo.','A total that omits the dates.'],'A single monthly median can hide variation by day; disaggregated data would show it.','reading-distribution','median-as-full-description'],
 ['A report says that 70% of complete applications were approved. Incomplete applications were not included.','What population does the 70% directly describe?','The complete applications included in the measure.',['All people who considered applying.','Every application, complete or incomplete.','All residents in the district.'],'The stated denominator is complete applications, so the result should not be generalized beyond it without evidence.','reading-denominator-scope','denominator-expansion'],
 ['Two districts show equal totals for completed cases. District A served 600 people; District B served 900.','What is needed to compare completion proportion?','The number completed divided by the relevant number served in each district.',['The district names in alphabetical order.','The totals only.','The number of staff titles.'],'Equal counts do not imply equal proportions when the served populations differ.','reading-normalized-comparison','equal-count-equal-rate'],
 ['The audit found that a new control was followed in 92% of sampled files. It did not inspect whether the control prevented errors.','What does the 92% measure?','Observed compliance with the control in the sample.',['The percentage of all errors prevented.','The legal validity of every file.','The control’s causal effect.'],'The measure concerns observed use, not the outcome or causal effectiveness.','reading-process-vs-outcome','process-compliance-as-effect'],
 ['An announcement says a service is available online. It does not state that every request can be completed online.','Which inference is justified?','An online service exists, but universal online completion is not established.',['Every request is fully online.','No in-person support exists.','The service is unavailable.'],'Availability does not imply that every case or step can be completed online.','reading-scope-qualifier','availability-as-universal'],
 ['A comparison shows fewer late cases after a reminder was introduced, but the deadline was also extended.','What should the analyst account for?','The extended deadline is a concurrent change that could affect lateness.',['The reminder must have caused the difference.','The reminder cannot have mattered.','The count is automatically invalid.'],'A simultaneous deadline change is a plausible alternative contributor.','reading-cointervention','single-cause-attribution'],
 ['A report states that a sample’s average score rose. The distribution became more uneven, with some scores falling.','What does the average fail to show alone?','How the scores changed across individuals or subgroups.',['Whether an average can be calculated.','Whether the reported mean rose.','Whether any scores exist.'],'An aggregate mean can rise while some observations fall; distribution matters.','reading-aggregate-vs-distribution','mean-as-everyone'],
 ['A policy summary says a deadline applies to “complete submissions.” It does not define when a submission becomes complete.','What should a reader avoid assuming?','That the summary alone resolves the meaning of complete.',['That the word complete appears.','That the deadline exists.','That more detail could clarify the rule.'],'The undefined term may be controlled by the underlying rule or procedure.','reading-defined-term','summary-as-full-rule'],
 ['A chart omits zero on its vertical axis, making a small difference look large.','Which check most directly evaluates that visual impression?','Inspect the vertical scale and compare the plotted values themselves.',['Check whether each series uses the same denominator.','Check whether the reporting periods have the same length.','Check whether the values describe counts or rates.'],'A truncated axis can magnify visual differences; the scale and underlying values show the size of the gap.','reading-chart-scale','visual-magnitude-as-data'],
 ['A team reports a favorable result but only for people who completed follow-up. Those lost to follow-up are not described.','What bias may affect the conclusion?','Attrition or nonresponse bias.',['A guarantee that results are false.','A unit-conversion error.','A filing-order problem.'],'People missing from follow-up may differ systematically from those retained.','reading-follow-up-coverage','completers-as-all'],
 ['A survey asks whether service was “good” but gives no definition and uses different wording at two branches.','What is the main measurement concern?','Responses may not measure the same construct consistently.',['The branches must have identical results.','The survey is a census by definition.','Wording cannot affect responses.'],'Different or undefined wording can alter what respondents interpret and answer.','reading-measurement-consistency','label-as-common-construct'],
 ['A memo claims a program is efficient because it served more people, but reports no staffing or cost.','What does the evidence not establish?','Efficiency relative to resources used.',['That the number served rose.','That the program existed.','That a comparison was made.'],'Efficiency relates output to inputs; output alone is insufficient.','reading-output-vs-efficiency','output-as-efficiency'],
 ['A report lists three reasons for delay but says the list came from interviews with five staff members.','What is a reasonable qualification?','The reasons reflect those interviews and may not exhaust all causes.',['The reasons apply to every case.','The interviews are necessarily useless.','No further evidence can be collected.'],'A small qualitative set can identify possibilities without establishing completeness or prevalence.','reading-qualitative-scope','examples-as-exhaustive'],
 ['A request’s processing time fell after a new workflow. The report compares the median, while the number of very long cases increased.','Which conclusion best fits both facts?','The typical wait fell, but the tail of long waits may have worsened.',['Every request became faster.','The median must be wrong.','Long waits cannot coexist with a lower median.'],'Median and tail behavior describe different parts of the distribution.','reading-median-tail','median-as-uniform'],
 ['The text says a new checklist “may reduce” incomplete submissions and calls for evaluation after six months.','What does may indicate?','A possibility or expected potential, not a guaranteed result.',['A proven universal effect.','A legal prohibition.','A completed evaluation.'],'The wording is cautious and the evaluation is still planned.','modal-verb-inference','possibility-as-certainty'],
 ['A summary says a service was “up to 40% faster” but gives no comparison cases.','Which conclusion is safest?','The wording reports a maximum claim, but does not establish a typical improvement or its cause.',['Every case was 40% faster.','The average improvement was exactly 40%.','The service change caused a 40% gain in every office.'],'“Up to” does not mean every or average case; without comparison details the typical effect and causal contribution remain unestablished.','reading-maximum-claim','maximum-as-typical'],
 ['A report states that a policy was followed in all inspected files, but the inspection sampled only one office.','What is the strongest warranted scope?','The policy was followed in the inspected sample at that office.',['The policy is followed in every office.','Every file nationwide was inspected.','The policy is effective.'],'The sample’s location and coverage limit the generalization.','reading-sample-scope','local-as-universal'],
 ['An office found that 12% of complaints involved one form, but that form was used in 60% of transactions.','What comparison may be more informative?','Complaint rates per transaction for each form type.',['Raw complaint counts without denominators.','The percentage of forms printed.','The form with the larger page count.'],'Different usage volume requires denominator-aware rates for fair comparison.','reading-exposure-denominator','count-without-exposure'],
 ['A report states that a revised instruction was associated with fewer errors, but the report does not specify when the error count was collected.','What should be clarified?','The measurement period and whether it is comparable before and after revision.',['The office’s street address only.','The color of the instruction.','Whether fewer is a comparative adjective.'],'A time frame and comparable measurement conditions are necessary for interpreting change.','reading-time-boundary','time-period-omitted'],
];
