/* ANALYTICAL: conditional logic, assumption testing, patterns, and data limits. */
$logicItems=[
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A request is released only if identity and authorization are both verified. Identity is verified, but authorization is pending. What follows?','The release condition is not yet fully satisfied.',['Identity verification alone requires immediate release.','Authorization is irrelevant.','The request has been permanently denied.'],'The stated rule requires both conditions; one remains pending.','necessary-conditions','one-condition-suffices'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','If a file is complete, it enters review. File J did not enter review. Under the rule, what follows?','File J was not complete.',['File J was complete.','Every file is incomplete.','The review system is invalid.'],'The contrapositive of complete → review is no review → not complete.','conditional-inference','affirming-consequent'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','Every urgent request receives a red flag. Request K has a red flag. What can be concluded?','The flag alone does not prove that K is urgent.',['K is certainly urgent.','No urgent request can be flagged.','K is already approved.'],'A sufficient condition does not become necessary merely because its result is observed.','one-way-conditional','affirming-consequent'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','All indexed records have a locator. No record without a locator is searchable. Record M has a locator. Which conclusion is warranted?','The premises do not establish whether M is indexed or searchable.',['M is indexed.','M is searchable.','M is not searchable.'],'Having a locator is stated as necessary for indexing, not sufficient; the premises also do not equate locator with searchability.','quantifier-scope','necessary-as-sufficient'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','No confidential file is placed on the public shelf. File A is on the public shelf. What follows?','File A is not confidential under the stated rule.',['File A is confidential.','All files are public.','The shelf is unauthorized.'],'The two categories are mutually exclusive under the premise.','categorical-exclusion','category-overreach'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','All approved reimbursements have a signed authorization. Reimbursement 21 has no signed authorization. What follows?','It is not approved under the stated rule.',['It is approved.','It is pending because it has no receipt.','All reimbursements are invalid.'],'A required condition is absent, so the stated approved category does not apply.','necessary-condition','missing-necessary-condition'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','Some complete applications are audited. Application R is complete. What is logically supported?','R may or may not be audited.',['R is audited.','No complete application is audited.','R is incomplete.'],'“Some” does not establish that this particular complete application is among them.','existential-quantifier','some-to-all'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A unit proposes a second-person check after transcription errors increased. Which assumption most directly supports the proposal?','A second check can detect at least some relevant transcription errors.',['Every error is caused by carelessness.','A second check eliminates all errors.','The original records are unnecessary.'],'The recommendation depends on the check addressing the identified failure mode.','recommendation-mechanism','intervention-guarantees-result'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A manager recommends adding a walk-in period to improve access. Which assumption is needed?','Some intended users cannot use the current service hours or channel.',['Every user prefers walk-in service.','The office has unlimited staff.','Access cannot be measured.'],'A new access period is relevant if current arrangements leave some users unable to participate.','access-assumption','universal-preference'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A report credits a new form with reducing errors, but a training program began the same week. Which assumption is needed for the report’s causal claim?','Training did not account for the observed reduction, or its contribution was separated.',['The form was printed in color.','Errors are always random.','The training had no participants.'],'A concurrent intervention is an alternative explanation that must be addressed.','causal-alternative','single-cause-attribution'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A survey reports a 9% satisfaction increase but gives no baseline or respondent count. What is the most useful request?','The starting value, respondent count, period, and calculation basis.',['A more positive headline.','Removal of the underlying records.','A conclusion that the service succeeded.'],'Without the base and sample information, the percentage is difficult to interpret.','percent-evidence-quality','percentage-without-base'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','Two offices show different average processing times, but one received more complex cases. What should be examined before attributing the difference to staff performance?','Whether case mix and measurement periods are comparable.',['Which office has newer furniture.','Whether the averages are printed in the same font.','Whether the faster office has a shorter name.'],'Differences in workload complexity can confound a simple performance comparison.','comparison-confounding','unadjusted-comparison'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A pilot at one site reports fewer missed appointments after reminders. Which follow-up would most strengthen a causal assessment?','Compare a suitable baseline or comparison group and track other changes.',['Repeat the same claim in the next report.','Exclude all people who missed an appointment.','Use the result as proof for every site.'],'A comparison and tracking of concurrent changes help distinguish intervention effects from alternatives.','causal-design','pilot-as-proof'],
 ['cse-data-trends','Completed requests rise from 48 to 55 to 63 over three successive weeks. Which statement is supported?','The reported weekly counts increased in each interval.',['The new policy caused the increase.','The increase will continue indefinitely.','Every office had the same trend.'],'The sequence establishes an observed increase, not a cause or forecast.','monotonic-trend','trend-to-cause'],
 ['cse-data-trends','A line item rises from 20 to 30, then falls to 25. Which description is accurate?','It ends above its initial value despite a decline in the last interval.',['It increases in every interval.','It ends below its starting value.','The final decline erases the earlier increase.'],'25 exceeds the initial 20; the second interval is a decline.','nonmonotonic-trend','last-change-as-total'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','Office A handled 96 cases in 8 days; Office B handled 110 in 10 days. Which had the higher daily average?','Office A.',['Office B.','They were equal.','The totals alone cannot be converted to rates.'],'A averages 12 per day; B averages 11 per day.','rate-comparison','total-as-rate'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A program’s count rises from 40 to 50. What is the percentage increase?','25%',['10%','20%','125%'],'The increase is 10; 10 ÷ 40 × 100 = 25%, using the original count as the base.','percent-change-interpretation','absolute-change-as-percent'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A service records 30 complaints among 600 transactions in one month and 24 among 400 in another. Which month has the higher complaint rate?','The second month: 6% versus 5%.',['The first month: 30% versus 24%.','The rates are equal.','Counts alone establish the first month has a higher rate.'],'30/600 = 5%; 24/400 = 6%. The second rate is higher despite fewer complaints.','rate-vs-count','count-rate-confusion'],
 ['cse-data-trends','A table shows 12, 18, 19, and 27 completed cases. What is the size of the largest increase between successive entries?','8 cases',['6 cases','1 case','15 cases'],'The successive increases are 6, 1, and 8 cases; the largest is 8.','interval-difference','compare-levels-not-changes',3,false,['operation'=>'largest_absolute_step','inputs'=>[12,18,19,27],'expected'=>8]],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','Index is to locator as calendar is to:','date.',['employee.','cabinet.','signature.'],'A locator identifies where a record can be found; a date identifies when an event occurs. The relation is tool-to-identifying-reference.','functional-analogy','surface-category-match'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','Archive is to preserve as filter is to:','screen.',['destroy.','duplicate.','approve.'],'An archive preserves records; a filter screens items according to a criterion.','verb-relation-analogy','opposite-action'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','Premise is to conclusion as evidence is to:','inference.',['deadline.','signature.','folder.'],'A conclusion is derived from premises; an inference is drawn from evidence.','reasoning-relation-analogy','object-association'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','Kilometer is to distance as hour is to:','time.',['speed.','volume.','weight.'],'A kilometer is a unit of distance; an hour is a unit of time.','unit-measure-analogy','quantity-unit-confusion'],
 ['analytical-analogy-structure','A checksum detects certain changes in a file. A reconciliation compares:','records or totals to identify and resolve discrepancies.',['a report’s tone to its audience.','a deadline to a public holiday only.','two unrelated documents for style.'],'The relationship is a method matched to its function: reconciliation surfaces differences in corresponding records or totals.','process-function-analogy','similar-word-not-function'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A document is accepted if it is signed and submitted before the deadline. It is signed but submitted late. What follows?','The stated acceptance condition is not met.',['It must be accepted because it is signed.','It must be accepted because it exists.','It is accepted unless the signature is missing.'],'Both stated conditions are required; timeliness is absent.','conjunctive-condition','one-condition-suffices'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','Every reviewed claim has a source. Claim B has no source. Under this rule, what follows?','Claim B has not been reviewed under the stated definition.',['Claim B is false.','Every unsourced claim is false.','Claim B is approved.'],'The premise connects review status with a source, not truth or falsity.','necessary-evidence','evidence-status-as-truth'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','If a receipt is invalid, the claim is returned. A claim was returned. What can be inferred?','The return alone does not show that its receipt was invalid.',['Its receipt was invalid.','All returned claims lack receipts.','The claim was paid.'],'The rule is one-way; a claim might be returned for another reason.','conditional-cause','affirming-consequent'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','Two offices interpret “complete submission” differently. Which evidence most directly tests whether a shared glossary improves consistency?','Compare classification agreement on the same sample before and after using the glossary.',['Count how many glossary copies were printed.','Ask only the glossary author whether it is clear.','Assume identical wording guarantees identical interpretation.'],'Applying both approaches to the same sample tests whether definitions improve agreement; printing or author opinion does not measure consistency.','glossary-consistency-test','document-existence-as-effect'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A before-and-after chart shows fewer delays after a new queue system. No information is given about demand volume. What limitation matters?','A change in demand could contribute to the observed delay difference.',['The chart cannot show any values.','The queue system must have failed.','Demand is irrelevant to waiting time.'],'Demand volume can affect delays and is an alternative explanation to consider.','missing-confounder','omitted-variable-ignored'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','A unit’s rate is 14 resolved cases per day for 5 days; another’s is 12 per day for 7 days. Which statement is accurate?','The first unit has the higher daily rate, but the second resolves more cases in total.',['The first has the higher total and rate.','The second has the higher daily rate and lower total.','Both total and rate are equal.'],'Totals are 70 and 84; rates are 14 and 12 per day.','rate-total-distinction','rate-equals-total'],
 ['cse-abstract-transformations','A filing code changes by swapping its first and last digits: 482 becomes 284. Applying the same operation to 731 gives:','137',['317','713','173'],'The first and last digits exchange positions; the middle digit stays fixed.','digit-position-transformation','middle-digit-moved'],
 ['cse-sequence-inference','A sequence begins 1, 2, 6, 24, 120. If each term is multiplied by the next positive integer, what is the next term?','720',['600','620','840'],'The multipliers are 2, 3, 4, and 5; multiply 120 by 6 to get 720.','factorial-growth','repeat-previous-multiplier'],
