 ['cse-logical-conditions','A file may be released only after identity and authorization are verified. Identity is verified; authorization is pending. What follows?','The stated release condition is not yet fully met.',['Identity alone requires release.','The file is permanently denied.','Authorization is irrelevant.'],'The rule requires both conditions; one remains pending.','two-condition-rule','one-condition-suffices'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','If a request is complete, it receives a tracking number. Request R has no tracking number. Assuming the rule was followed, what follows?','Request R was not complete.',['Request R was complete.','Every request is incomplete.','The tracking system caused the omission.'],'This is the contrapositive of complete → tracking number.','contrapositive-inference','affirming-consequent'],
 ['analytical-conclusion-validity','All approved claims have an authorization record. Claim M has an authorization record. What is justified?','The record alone does not establish that Claim M was approved.',['Claim M was approved.','No approved claim has a record.','Claim M was paid.'],'A necessary feature of approval does not prove that every claim with it is approved.','necessary-not-sufficient','affirming-consequent'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','No confidential file is placed on the public shelf. File K is on the public shelf. What follows under the stated rule?','File K is not confidential.',['File K is confidential.','All files are public.','The shelf is unauthorized.'],'The rule excludes confidential files from the public shelf.','categorical-exclusion','category-overlap'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','Some requests with receipts are incomplete. Request J has a receipt. What can be concluded?','Request J may be complete or incomplete.',['Request J is incomplete.','Every request with a receipt is incomplete.','No request with a receipt is complete.'],'An existential statement about some requests does not classify this particular request.','existential-scope','some-to-all'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','An office proposes assigning a unique code to each request to prevent records from being mixed up. Which assumption most directly supports the proposal?','The code is assigned consistently and remains linked to the correct request throughout processing.',['Every request has the same applicant name.','A unique code removes the need to verify source information.','Codes may be reused for different open requests.'],'The proposal depends on a stable, correctly linked identifier distinguishing each request.','unique-code-control','identifier-linkage-assumed'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','After translated instructions are added, completion errors fall. Which follow-up best assesses whether language access contributed to the change?','Compare error patterns by users’ preferred language across comparable periods and document other process changes.',['Attribute the entire decline to translation without checking other changes.','Count translated pages but not completion outcomes.','Compare unrelated offices with different forms and deadlines.'],'Stratification and comparable periods help test the proposed pathway while recording alternative changes.','translation-outcome-evaluation','single-change-attribution'],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A unit proposes translating a service notice after residents repeatedly misunderstand its instructions. Which assumption most directly supports the proposal?','Some intended readers may understand the required steps better in the languages added.',['Every reader prefers the same language.','Translation guarantees that the underlying instructions are correct.','The notice needs no review after translation.'],'The proposed change can address an access barrier if language comprehension contributes to the reported misunderstandings.','language-access-assumption','translation-as-guarantee'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A hypothetical routing rule sends complete applications due within five working days to a priority lane; incomplete applications are returned for completion. File P is complete and due in four working days. Where does it go?','The priority lane.',['The ordinary lane because every file must wait.','Return for completion.','The rule gives no route for a complete application.'],'P meets both stated conditions for priority and is not incomplete.','deadline-routing-rule','ignoring-conjunctive-conditions'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A pilot at one office reports fewer delayed files after a reminder was introduced. What is the strongest next step for assessing the reminder’s effect?','Compare a suitable baseline or comparison and record concurrent changes.',['Repeat the claim in another memo.','Assume every office will have the same result.','Exclude files that remained delayed.'],'A comparison and information about other changes help assess alternatives and generalizability.','causal-assessment','pilot-as-proof'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A survey response rate is high, but an entire group of contract employees was excluded. What limitation remains?','Coverage bias may remain because an affected group was not sampled.',['A high response rate guarantees representation.','Contract employees can never affect the result.','All responses are invalid.'],'Response rate does not repair exclusion from the sampling frame.','survey-coverage','response-rate-as-representativeness'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A report says a service improved based on one unusually fast case. What is the main evidence problem?','One case may not represent typical performance or comparable conditions.',['Service time cannot be measured.','One observation always establishes a trend.','A fast case proves the measure is false.'],'A single case without representative or comparable evidence is weak support for a general claim.','anecdote-and-comparability','anecdote-as-trend'],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A chart shows fewer complaints after a change, but the reporting channel also changed. What should be checked?','Whether complaints through all channels were counted consistently before and after.',['Whether the later period covered fewer calendar days.','Whether complaint severity was identical in every case.','Whether the change was announced in a memo.'],'A changed measurement channel can alter recorded counts without changing the underlying event; the time window and reporting coverage must also be comparable.','measurement-change','recorded-count-as-event-count'],
 ['cse-data-trends','Reported totals are 42, 47, 45, and 53. Which statement is accurate?','The total fluctuates but ends above its starting value.',['It increases in every interval.','It ends below its start.','It rises by five in every interval.'],'There is a decline from 47 to 45, while 53 is greater than 42.','nonmonotonic-series','monotonic-assumption'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','There are 30 complaints among 600 transactions in one period and 24 among 400 in another. Why can the period with fewer complaints have a higher rate?','Its denominator is smaller relative to the complaint count.',['Rates depend only on the count.','A lower count always means a lower rate.','The denominator is not part of a rate.'],'30/600 is 5%; 24/400 is 6%, so the lower count has the higher proportion.','rate-denominator','count-rate-confusion'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','Two offices report the same total completed cases, but one operated twice as many days. What comparison better reflects daily pace?','Completed cases per operating day for each office.',['The totals alone.','Office names in alphabetical order.','Subtract days from cases without units.'],'A rate using operating days adjusts for the different exposure periods.','normalize-by-time','total-as-rate'],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','A measure rises from 20 to 30 and later falls to 25. Which description is accurate?','It ends above its initial level, although it declined in the final interval.',['It rose in every interval.','It ends below the initial level.','The later decline erases the earlier observations.'],'The final value 25 exceeds the initial 20, while the last step is downward.','net-vs-interval','last-change-as-total'],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A report states that 72 of 90 resolved cases were complete. What is needed to interpret this as a rate for all cases received?','Whether 90 includes all cases in the period and how “resolved” is defined.',['The report’s color scheme.','The names of employees only.','Whether 72 is even.'],'A rate needs a clear denominator and outcome definition.','denominator-and-definition','rate-without-population'],
 ['cse-abstract-transformations','A code rule moves each letter forward two places: A→C and D→F. What does M become?','O',['N','P','Q'],'Advancing two alphabet positions from M gives O.','two-step-letter-map','one-step-shift'],
 ['cse-abstract-transformations','A three-character code is reversed, retaining all characters: 307 becomes 703. What does 052 become?','250',['520','205','025'],'Reverse the three positions: 0-5-2 becomes 2-5-0, displayed as 250.','position-reversal','leading-position-loss'],
 ['cse-syllogistic-reasoning','All complete applications have a receipt. Some applications with receipts are under appeal. What is certain about a complete application?','It has a receipt, but whether it is under appeal is not established.',['It must be under appeal.','Every application with a receipt is complete.','No complete application can be under appeal.'],'The universal premise gives every complete application a receipt; the existential statement does not identify those applications as complete.','overlapping-sets-not-established','existence-as-universal-link'],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A file is retained if it is under appeal or subject to an audit hold. It is neither. What follows?','Neither listed ground establishes retention; another rule could still apply.',['It must be destroyed immediately.','It is retained under the stated grounds.','An audit hold exists.'],'The stated grounds are absent, but the premise does not rule out other authority.','disjunctive-rule','listed-ground-as-exhaustive-law'],
];
foreach($analyticalExtension as $r)$add(['concept'=>$r[0],'stem'=>$r[1],'answer'=>$r[2],'wrong'=>$r[3],'why'=>$r[4],'family'=>$r[5],'misconception'=>$r[6],'depth'=>in_array($r[0],['cse-evidence-strength','analytical-assumption-identification'],true)?4:3]);

