 ['cse-contextual-inference','A notice says a request is “complete for initial review” but still awaits identity verification. What follows?','Initial review may proceed, but the request has not passed identity verification.',['The request is finally approved.','Identity verification is unnecessary.','The request is incomplete in every respect.'],'The qualifier limits what complete means here; a separate verification step remains.','context-limited-completeness','qualifier-ignored',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A report states that errors fell among 40 files rechecked after training. It does not say how the files were selected. What is the main limitation?','The result may not represent all files if the rechecked files were selected non-randomly.',['The error count cannot be reported.','Training had no possible effect.','All files had errors.'],'Selection method affects how far the observed result can be generalized.','reading-sample-selection','sample-as-population',4],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A memo lists three reasons a form is returned: missing signature, unreadable date, and absent attachment. Which sentence best summarizes it?','Returns occurred for three specified documentation problems.',['Every returned form had all three problems.','The office rejects all applications.','The memo recommends replacing the form.'],'The summary preserves the three listed reasons without adding a universal claim.','reading-list-summary','list-as-joint-condition',3],
 ['cse-contextual-inference','The report says “processing time declined for complete applications; incomplete applications were excluded.” What cannot be concluded?','That processing time declined for all applications received.',['That the report concerns complete applications.','That incomplete applications were excluded.','That the reported subgroup had a decline.'],'The passage limits the claim to complete applications.','reading-population-boundary','subgroup-as-universal',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A pilot met its target in April and May but missed it in June. Which summary is most accurate?','It met the target in two of the three reported months.',['It met the target throughout the quarter.','It missed the target in every month.','The target was never measured.'],'The statement counts the months precisely and avoids an all-or-none summary.','reading-quantifier-summary','most-as-all',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A notice says walk-in service is available “while daily capacity remains.” The daily limit has been reached. Which conclusion follows?','The notice does not guarantee further walk-in service that day.',['Every walk-in must still be served.','Walk-in service is permanently discontinued.','The capacity limit is irrelevant.'],'The condition “while capacity remains” is no longer met.','reading-condition-application','conditional-as-unconditional',3],
 ['cse-paragraph-organization','A paragraph explains why a count changed. Which order is clearest?','State the change, compare the underlying records, then give a supported explanation or note what remains unknown.',['Give a cause first, then look for confirming records.','List unrelated totals before stating the change.','Recommend a response without describing the evidence.'],'The sequence moves from issue to evidence and then to a bounded conclusion.','organization-evidence-before-conclusion','conclusion-before-evidence',4],
 ['cse-paragraph-cohesion','A writer states that the total number of requests rose while the completion rate fell. Which transition best introduces the explanation that the incoming workload grew faster than completions?','because',['nevertheless','for example','similarly'],'Because introduces the causal mechanism offered for the contrasting measures.','transition-cause-mechanism','contrast-for-cause',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A paragraph says an office adopted a checklist, but returned-file counts were already falling before adoption. Which detail most weakens the claim that the checklist caused the decline?','The decline began before the checklist was introduced.',['The checklist has numbered steps.','The office kept a copy of the checklist.','The report uses monthly totals.'],'A pre-existing downward trend is an alternative explanation for the later decline.','reading-temporal-order','after-as-cause',4],
 ['cse-contextual-inference','An analyst writes, “The finding is consistent with the proposal, though it does not distinguish the proposal from two other explanations.” What is the careful reading?','The finding does not contradict the proposal, but it is not unique evidence for it.',['The proposal is proven.','The proposal is disproven.','The other explanations are impossible.'],'Consistency is weaker than exclusive or causal confirmation.','qualified-evidence-reading','compatibility-as-proof',4],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A bulletin says applications increased after a new information session. It reports neither attendance nor the applicants’ reasons for applying. What is the best conclusion?','Applications increased afterward, but the bulletin does not establish that the session caused the increase.',['The session caused every additional application.','No one attended the session.','The increase was unrelated to the session.'],'The passage reports timing but lacks evidence isolating cause.','reading-causal-limits','sequence-as-cause',4],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A service note says the average wait was 18 minutes and the median was 9 minutes. What is a reasonable interpretation?','Some longer waits may have raised the average above the median.',['Every visitor waited exactly 18 minutes.','The median must be wrong.','No visitor waited less than 9 minutes.'],'A mean above the median can occur with a right-skewed distribution, though the note does not reveal every wait.','reading-mean-median','average-as-individual',4],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A report compares two offices but uses counts from different-length reporting periods. What should a careful reader request before comparing totals?','Comparable time periods or a clearly defined rate.',['A larger font for the table.','The office names in alphabetical order.','A statement that totals never change.'],'Different exposure periods can make raw totals misleading.','reading-comparison-period','unequal-periods-as-equal',3],
 ['cse-paragraph-organization','A recommendation is followed by “The proposed check catches missing signatures before release.” What role does that sentence most likely play?','It supplies a reason supporting the recommendation.',['It contradicts the recommendation.','It changes the subject to a new office.','It states an unrelated historical detail.'],'The sentence describes a mechanism by which the recommendation could help.','paragraph-support-function','mechanism-as-example-only',3],
 ['cse-contextual-inference','A memo calls a draft “tentative pending review.” Which interpretation best preserves both qualifiers?','The draft is not final, and its status depends on a review that remains outstanding.',['The draft is final but may be edited later.','The draft has been rejected.','The review has no bearing on the draft.'],'Tentative and pending jointly indicate that review has not yet settled the draft.','double-qualification','tentative-as-rejection',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A table reports 80 completed cases out of 100 received. A note says 20 additional cases were still open. Which denominator best answers “What share of received cases were completed?”','100',['80','20','120'],'The question asks for the share of cases received; the denominator is 100, including the open cases.','reading-denominator-selection','resolved-only-denominator',3],
 ['cse-paragraph-cohesion','The first sentence says a sample excludes remote offices. The next sentence makes a claim about every office. Which revision best improves cohesion and accuracy?','Limit the claim to the offices represented in the sample.',['Keep “every office” because the sample is large.','Remove the sample description.','Add a transition word without changing the claim.'],'The scope of the conclusion should match the population represented by the sample.','scope-consistent-summary','sample-to-universe',4],
 ['cse-contextual-inference','A report calls the increase “modest but persistent across four months.” Which detail would best support the word persistent?','The measure rose in each of the four consecutive months.',['The final month had the largest value.','The report used a blue chart.','The measure was compared with an unrelated agency.'],'Persistence refers to continuation across the reported periods.','trend-word-in-context','endpoint-as-persistence',3],
 ['cse-reading-comprehension','A survey finds that 62% of respondents favor a proposal. The report gives no response rate. What important limitation remains?','Nonrespondents may differ from respondents, so representativeness is uncertain.',['The reported percentage cannot be 62%.','Every nonrespondent opposed the proposal.','The survey proves the proposal is effective.'],'Without response information, nonresponse bias cannot be assessed.','reading-nonresponse','respondents-as-everyone',4],
 ['cse-grammar-application','Which sentence correctly keeps the subject and verb together despite the intervening phrase?','The schedule of field visits changes each Monday.',['The schedule of field visits change each Monday.','The schedule, along with the field visits, change each Monday.','The schedule of field visits changing each Monday.'],'The head subject schedule is singular, so it takes changes.','agreement-head-subject','nearest-noun-agreement',3],
 ['cse-grammar-application','Which sentence uses a restrictive clause to identify the records being discussed?','Files that lack a control number require a second check.',['Files, that lack a control number, require a second check.','Files which lacks a control number require a second check.','Files whom lack a control number require a second check.'],'The clause identifies which files; that is used for the restrictive clause in this sentence.','restrictive-clause','comma-changes-scope',3],
 ['cse-pronoun-reference','Which revision makes clear that the supervisor—not the clerk—approved the correction?','The clerk prepared the correction, and the supervisor approved it.',['The clerk spoke with the supervisor after she approved the correction.','After reviewing it, the correction was approved.','The supervisor told the clerk that she should approve it.'],'The revision names the actor and keeps the object reference clear.','pronoun-actor-clarity','ambiguous-antecedent',3],
