 ['analytical-assumption-identification','A service manager proposes a separate intake lane for applications nearing a filing deadline. Which assumption most directly supports the proposal?','Staff can identify the relevant deadline and route those applications without preventing other required work.',['Every application should use the priority lane.','A separate lane eliminates the total workload.','The filing deadline has no effect on routing decisions.'],'The proposal depends on a workable way to identify time-sensitive applications and route them while maintaining the rest of the service.','deadline-triage-mechanism','priority-lane-as-capacity',4],
 ['analytical-assumption-identification','An office proposes consolidating two service counters to reduce staffing costs. Which assumption most directly supports the proposal?','The remaining counter can handle combined demand without unacceptable delays or loss of access.',['Staff costs are the only relevant service outcome.','Every request takes the same amount of time.','Fewer counters automatically reduce total demand.'],'The proposal depends on the remaining service capacity being adequate after consolidation.','service-capacity-consolidation','cost-as-only-outcome',4],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','Office A reports fewer overdue cases after a new process, but it also received fewer cases. Which comparison is more informative?','Compare overdue-case rates using a consistent denominator and comparable time periods.',['Compare raw overdue counts only.','Assume fewer incoming cases cannot affect the overdue count.','Compare office names and staffing titles.'],'A workload-adjusted rate and consistent periods help separate performance from a change in incoming volume.','backlog-denominator-comparison','count-as-performance',4],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A small pilot reports fewer errors after a new checklist. Which additional evidence would best strengthen a causal claim?','A suitable comparison and information about other changes during the pilot.',['A second memo repeating the same conclusion.','A guarantee that every office will respond identically.','Exclusion of cases where errors continued.'],'Comparison and concurrent-change information help assess alternative explanations.','causal-evidence-design','pilot-as-proof',4],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A survey has a high response rate but excludes an entire group affected by the policy. What limitation remains?','Coverage bias may remain because the group was absent from the sampling frame.',['High response guarantees representation.','The excluded group cannot affect the result.','All responses are automatically invalid.'],'A high response rate among those sampled cannot repair an omitted population group.','sampling-frame-coverage','response-as-representativeness',4],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','A complaint total falls, but the earlier figure included phone and mail while the later figure counts web reports only. What correction is needed before comparing the totals?','Use consistent channel coverage for both periods or qualify the comparison as non-equivalent.',['Treat the later web count as the total for every channel.','Assume the channel change could not affect the count.','Compare the raw totals without noting the measurement change.'],'Changing which reporting channels are counted creates a measurement break; harmonize coverage or state that the totals are not directly comparable.','measurement-channel-shift','single-channel-as-all-reports',4],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A monthly mean processing time falls after one exceptionally short batch, while the median is unchanged. What should an analyst examine before describing a typical improvement?','The distribution and composition of cases, because the mean may shift while the median stays stable.',['Only the single shortest case.','Whether the total number of words in the report changed.','Whether an unchanged median proves that no individual case changed.'],'Mean and median respond differently to distributions and case mix; both should be interpreted with the underlying observations.','mean-median-case-mix','mean-as-every-case',3],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A table shows resolved cases by counter: Counter A changes from 20 to 30, while Counter B changes from 30 to 20. The total is 50 in both periods. What does the unchanged total conceal?','The distribution shifted between counters even though the aggregate total stayed the same.',['Both counters stayed unchanged.','The total increased by 10.','No change occurred in any component.'],'Offsetting component changes can leave an aggregate unchanged while the underlying distribution shifts.','aggregate-hides-offsetting-changes','aggregate-as-no-change',3],
 ['cse-data-interpretation','A summary combines totals from offices with different staffing and operating days. What information would make a workload comparison more meaningful?','A consistently defined rate with an appropriate exposure denominator.',['The largest total only.','Office names in alphabetical order.','A new total without its time period.'],'Rates can adjust for different staffing or operating time when definitions are comparable.','normalization-choice','raw-total-as-fair-comparison',4],
 ['cse-data-trends','Values are 17, 21, 20, and 26 across four periods. Which statement is accurate?','The series declined once and ended above its starting value.',['It increased in every interval.','It ended below its starting value.','It rose by four in every interval.'],'The values fall from 21 to 20 and finish at 26, above 17.','mixed-trend-description','monotonic-assumption',3],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','Office A reports a higher total than Office B, but Office A served twice as many people. Which comparison is fairest for service frequency?','Compare events per person served for each office.',['Compare the totals alone.','Compare each office with its own earlier month instead of using a population denominator.','Assume the higher count is always worse.'],'A per-person rate accounts for different service volumes when the event definitions match.','denominator-matching','count-as-rate',3],
 ['analytical-data-comparison','A measure rose from 12 to 18 and then fell to 16. Which description is correct?','It ended above its initial value but declined in the last interval.',['It rose in every interval.','It ended below its starting value.','The final decline erases the first change.'],'The final value 16 exceeds 12, while the last movement was downward.','endpoint-versus-last-step','last-change-as-total',3],
 ['cse-evidence-strength','Two explanations fit the same observed pattern. What is the best next analytical step?','Identify a comparison or new observation that would distinguish their predictions.',['Choose the more familiar explanation.','Treat both explanations as proven.','Discard the observed pattern.'],'A discriminating observation can provide evidence between competing explanations.','competing-hypothesis-test','familiarity-as-evidence',4],
 ['cse-logical-conditions','A form is valid only if signed, unless a rule grants an exception. It is unsigned and no exception is stated. What follows?','The stated condition for validity is not met on the available facts.',['It is valid because no one objected.','It is void for every purpose.','An exception must have been granted.'],'The stated necessary condition—signature or an established exception—is absent; no broader legal consequence is inferred.','exception-scope','exception-assumed',4],
